          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"ibsen-et-dukkehjem-003dfd3f3df5ffc6\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Rank's elliptical question—“That I have loved you every bit as deeply as anyone else?”—revoices and confirms his earlier confession. Its core force is a strong avowal presented as true, now framed as a challenge to Nora's calling his conduct horrid. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and the measured, retrospective formulation supplies no evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The question revoices a confession made moments earlier, but both the revoiced wording and the original disclosure are predominantly truth-conditional avowals.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Rank directly addresses Nora through an elliptical interrogative that revoices and clarifies his immediately preceding confession of love.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rank has disclosed his love after implying that he, like Torvald, would gladly die for Nora. When Nora calls his action horrid, Rank asks whether loving her was what was horrid; Nora explicitly replies that the problem was not his love but his telling her. This contrast foregrounds the truth-conditional content of his avowal while showing that the socially disruptive disclosure, rather than the emotional state itself, causes her objection. His surrounding offers of total assistance indicate devotion but do not turn this particular love utterance into an undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“RANK (reiser seg). At jeg har elsket Dem fullt så inderlig som noen annen? Var det stygt?”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person perfect construction explicitly presents Rank's deep love for Nora as true and rhetorically asks whether that state was objectionable.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Rank says Nora now knows his feelings; immediately afterward Nora answers, “Nei, men at De går hen og sier meg det” (“No, but that you should go and tell me”).\",\"supports\":\"Nora's distinction between his loving her and his disclosing it confirms that the core utterance revoices an avowal of emotional state rather than primarily performing a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rank earlier says he would gladly give his life for Nora and later says he is at her disposal “med liv og sjel.”\",\"supports\":\"The context establishes intense devotion, but those separate statements carry the undertaking-like implications; they do not provide independent performative or expressive/reflexive force for the highlighted wording.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative perfect, syntactically main-or-embedded, and retained as a valid explicit I-love-you equivalent.\",\"supports\":\"The grammatical and occurrence metadata corroborate reading the highlighted clause as an explicit retrospective avowal embedded in an elliptical question.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The interrogative revoicing and socially consequential context require contextual explanation but create no residual core force outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
